//
// `@FR-H-RefHome` — a holder minted again by its own literal in a loop pass keeps the record
// its field names by a copy into its store when the record's pass ends
// (`2621-a-holder-re-minted-in-a-loop-keeps-the-record-its-field-names.loft`).  A copy of the
// holder taken BEFORE that, in the same pass, still names the record where it was built: here
// a vector element, which has no fixed place to copy into, so nothing keeps the record for it
// and the copy is refused.  Before, it compiled and read null for every element but the last
// on both backends (loft#2621); a copy-out that re-pointed only the holder left the element
// reading the freed store.
//
// Hand-computed: the copy `v += [a]` is refused; nothing runs.
// @falsified-at: hand-measured (control: the installed main build 2026.11.20261121) — interpret
//   and native compile it and print `null null 9 9` for `3 6 9 9`.
//   CHANNEL: the refusal.
//   HOLDS: the same program with `v` holding records minted once (`a.doc = d` on one `a`) is
//   the place_own shape, kept and right on both trees.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: the `doc` field inside `v` (copied from `a`) would outlive the record it names
struct Doc { name: text, n: integer }
struct Pin { label: text, doc: reference<Doc>? }

fn main() {
  a = Pin { label: "none" };
  v: vector<Pin> = [];
  for i in 1..4 {
    d = Doc { name: "pin{i}", n: i * 3 };
    a = Pin { label: "p{i}", doc: d };
    v += [a];
  }
  println("{v[0].doc.n} {v[1].doc.n} {v[2].doc.n} {a.doc.n}");
}
